{"name":"The Wild Apothecary","hex":"#118844","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"wild","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Untamed; not domesticated.","Especially, being of the wild type: being of an unbroken ancestral line of undomesticated animals, as opposed to being feral, being an undomesticated animal whose ancestors were domesticated."]},{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["Inaccurately; not on target.","Intended to be synchronized with film or video but recorded separately."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wild"},{"term":"apothecary","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Synonym of pharmacist: a person who sells medicine, especially  one who made and sold their own medicines in the medieval or early modern eras.","Synonym of pharmacy: an apothecary's shop, a drugstore."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/apothecary"}],"entries":[{"title":"Apothecary","blocks":[{"text":"Apothecary is an archaic English term for a medical professional who formulates and dispenses materia medica ('medicine') to physicians, surgeons and patients. The modern terms pharmacist and, in British English, chemist have taken over this role."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Apothecary15thcentury_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Apothecary15thcentury_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apothecary","wikidata":"Q1131236"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Apothicaire.jpg/500px-Apothicaire.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Apothicaire.jpg/500px-Apothicaire.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A 15th-century French apothecary (at right)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AApothicaire.jpg"},{"srcset":"ht